    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey can u explain how lactic acid actually works in your muscles during high intensity training? like why does it make them burn and how does the body get rid of it after the workout is over?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Lactic acid is produced when muscle cells break down glucose without enough oxygen, turning it into pyruvate and then into lactate while also generating a small amount of ATP for quick energy. The “burn” you feel comes from the buildup of hydrogen ions (H⁺) that accompany lactate, which lower the pH in the muscle and make the contractile proteins work less efficiently. After the workout, lactate is shuttled out of the muscle into the blood, where the liver can reconvert it to glucose (the Cori cycle) or the heart and other oxidative muscles can oxidize it back to pyruvate for energy, while the extra H⁺ are buffered by bicarbonate and eventually exhaled as CO₂. This clearance process restores normal pH and eliminates the burning sensation.",
      "source": "target",
